# --- Settings, story items, notes, behaviours, quest ----------------------------------------------------------------
info title=Beacon Saddle
info author=Custom Islands
info description=Two sea stacks and a saddle between them, an iron beacon tower turning its red light, a radio hut repeating one pulse - and three cold campfires. The first island of The End of the Ocean.
island on.arrive=message||Two rock stacks, a green saddle between them, and an iron tower with a red light turning. The Receiver ticks in time with it.

story pulse|Pulse recording|Placeable_Reciever|A recording of the beacon's pulse, taken off the radio set in the hut on Beacon Saddle. It repeats every eight seconds - and it points south.
story letter|Letter to the Well|Note_Paper|The beacon keeper's letter, sealed, for MARA WREN, COMMAND WELL, CONCORD ENGINE. Returned nine years running: NO SUCH POST. Somebody should deliver it.

note pilgrimlog Pilgrims' log | PILGRIMS' LOG - Ines writing, by the middle fire\n\nThree of us. Ines, Hedda, Teo. South.\n\nWe came because of the pulse. Everyone on the rafts has heard it at night on the low bands - eight seconds, over and over, like a heart that forgot how to stop. Under the Hadal domes we heard it through the walls for years and called it the Hum. Teo says it is a machine. Hedda says it is a place. I think it is someone, and I have not said so out loud.\n\nThe keeper of this light is gone. His bed in the hut is made, his cup washed, his boots missing. A man who washes his cup before he leaves means to come back - or wants you to think he does.\n\nWe leave the fires. Whoever comes next: the radio set in the hut keeps the pulse. Take it with you, and do not listen to it alone at night.
note bearing Bearing card | BEARING CARD - pinned to a post, in Hedda's square capitals\n\nPulse strongest SOUTH by the hut's needle. Not south-ish. South, to the degree, every night for a week.\n\nThree kilometres on, the Receiver picks up a second voice, lower, like a pump. Ines heard it humming through the hull and went pale. I have seen reefs for forty years at sea and none of them hum.\n\nIf you are reading this you are behind us, and that is fine. Somebody should be. Bring water, bring rope, and look for the reef that grows in straight lines. That is where we will be, or where we were.\n\n- H. Brask, captain of nothing in particular
note towerchest Beacon tower | Wedged between the legs: aerial plans in a tin tube.
note radiochest Radio hut | Receiver plans, rolled round a spare valve.
loot towerchest Blueprint_Antenna*1;Blueprint_Recycler*1;Scrap*3;Bolt*2
prop towerchest loot.refill=0
loot radiochest Blueprint_Reciever*1;Blueprint_HeadLight*1;Battery*1;CircuitBoard*1
prop radiochest loot.refill=0

prop radioset obj.name=radioset
prop radioset beh.use=Record the pulse off the radio set
prop radioset if.use=!signal|pulse
prop radioset on.use=signal||pulse\ngive||story:pulse*1\nmessage||You let the pulse play once onto a spare reel. Under the beeps, very faint, an old man's voice: Calling. Calling. Answer me.\nsound||event:/raft/radio/cassette/cassette01
prop radioset else.use=message||The pulse is recorded. The set keeps repeating it to nobody.

note cavechest Cave chest | A pilgrim's blanket. Scratched in the lid: THE FIRST IS 7.
loot cavechest Rope*2;Bolt*2;CircuitBoard*1;Battery*1
prop cavechest loot.refill=0
note keeperpage Keeper's last page | THE KEEPER'S LAST PAGE - weighted down with a stone in the doorway\n\nThe roof went in the second storm. I sleep in the hut by the tower now, beside the set, so I hear every beat.\n\nThe pulse never misses one. I timed it against the mail plane's clock before the plane came down: eight seconds, to the tick. My wife Mara went south on the Station run to keep the great engine, and the pulse is the engine calling its crew home. That is what I tell myself. That is what I tell you.\n\nI found a reel in the tower long ago and never told the others what was on it. Some things a keeper keeps.\n\nI am going south to answer her myself. Whoever lights this lamp after me: my strongbox holds the card that opens the doors down there. The last is 1. Mind it well.\n\nOn the back, pressed hard into the paper: 741.
note planelog Mail plane log | MAIL PLANE LOG - the last page, written sideways\n\nCourse south for the Station run. Engine coughing since the second stop. I have told it that coughing is not a flight plan.\n\nThe Receiver needle swung hard south and stayed there, like a dog that has seen the butcher. Something down there is much louder than a beacon.\n\nPutting her down on the saddle's east slope. Aim: the grass. Result: also the grass, mostly.\n\nMail bags in the crate. One letter in the old keeper's hand, for MARA WREN, COMMAND WELL, CONCORD ENGINE. He gave it me every run for nine years and every run it came back: NO SUCH POST. Somebody deliver it. I am tired of carrying it.\n\nPencilled on the back, in the keeper's hand: the second is 4.
note goatchest Goatherd's chest | A goatherd's chest, its lid tied down with rope.
loot goatchest Rope*3;Plank*6;Nail*8;Wool*2
note keepertally Keeper's tally | BEACON KEEPER'S TALLY - in a tin under a stone\n\nDay 1 of the pulse: logged it. Three beats, a pause, three beats. Mara's station, if the bearing is right. She always did like a tidy signal.\n\nDay 40: still there. Nobody answers on 500. I answer anyway. I tell her about the hens.\n\nDay 300: the relief boat did not come. I keep the light turning anyway. Somebody out there must be listening.\n\nDay 2000: I have stopped counting days and started counting beats. It is easier. You cannot miss a day if you count beats.\n\nDay - never mind the day. The pulse is still here. So am I. Calling, calling.\n\n- S. Wren, keeper
# (deep pass 10-24: the climb and the dive)
story bell|Pilgrims' bell|Placeable_Lantern_Metal|A small brass bell the pilgrims hung in their caravan. It rang every time the pulse came. The lightkeeper's locker on the Old Light takes it.
story chainkey|Chain key|Bolt|A heavy key on a ring, stamped CONCORD WELL. One of the five that open the chained door of the Command Well, deep under the calm circle.
note bsdivechest Pilgrims' caravan | Under the bunk: the pilgrims' bell, tied with Hedda's ribbon.
loot bsdivechest story:bell*1;Rope*2;Scrap*3;Bolt*2
prop bsdivechest loot.refill=0
note bstop Lightkeeper's locker | The bell fits the lid. A chain key, and a spyglass aimed south.
loot bstop story:chainkey*1;TitaniumOre*2;Battery*1;Nail*12
prop bstop loot.refill=0
prop bstop obj.name=bstop
prop bstop if.open=has|story:bell|1
prop bstop else.open=message||The lightkeeper's locker. The lid has a bell-shaped hollow in it - something has to sit there first.
note bsstash Old Light stash | Spare fuses, and a pencilled line: THE BELL IS IN THE CISTERN.
loot bsstash Battery*1;CircuitBoard*1;Bolt*3
prop bsstash loot.refill=0
note bsdivesecret Caravan glovebox | A torn-out glovebox with Teo's tobacco tin in it. Empty, naturally.
loot bsdivesecret MetalIngot*2;Rope*2;Nail*6
prop bsdivesecret loot.refill=0
note bscairn Summit cairn | A cairn on the peak beside the Old Light, a note in a bottle:\n\nFrom up here you can see it. Far south a dark square in the flat water, chained shut, and round it a ring where the waves will not go.\n\nI sat here an hour with the radio on my knees. Between the beats there is a voice - an old man, saying the same word over and over. Calling. Calling. Hedda says it is the tape stretching. Teo says it is the wind. I know a voice when I hear one; I grew up under the sea, where voices are all you have.\n\nTomorrow we follow the needle to the straight reef. Whoever you are, follow us. - I.
